{
  "id": "5-11-defender-6-lab-a-spawner-for-the-waves",
  "title": "5.11.6 Lab: A Spawner for the waves",
  "description": "The waves move into a Spawner class: ready() says when the next wave is due and makeWave() builds it. A restart becomes new Spawner(), and Enemy counts the enemies it has built with a static counter.",
  "type": "lesson",
  "estimateMins": 22,
  "category": "Unit 2: moSHion: Applied Game Development",
  "unit": "5.11 Project: Defender, Part C",
  "preview": "moshion",
  "week": 12,
  "slos": [
    "SLO-2",
    "SLO-3"
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "title": "ready()",
      "instructions": "The new `Spawner` keeps `wave` and `pause`. `ready(enemiesLeft)` is the job `nextWave()` did in 5.10.7 (Lab: Waves), turned into a question. Answer `false` while `enemiesLeft` is above 0; otherwise count `this.pause` down by 1 and answer whether it has reached 0.",
      "hints": [
        "`if (enemiesLeft > 0) return false;` comes first: the pause only runs on a clear field.",
        "The last line can return a comparison: `return this.pause <= 0;`"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "title": "makeWave()",
      "instructions": "`makeWave()` is `spawnWave()` moved in, with `this.` in front of the spawner's own numbers. Add 1 to `this.wave`, set `this.pause` to 90 for the next gap, and work out `const speed = 0.5 + this.wave * 0.25;` the way `spawnWave()` worked out `fallSpeed`. Then build the wave into a new array, `const wave = [];`, with the same loop as `spawnWave()`, pushing `new Enemy(x, y, hp, speed)` for each one. Return the array at the end.",
      "hints": [
        "Only the push changes: `wave.push(new Enemy(40 + floor(random(8)) * 45, -20 - i * 50, hp, speed));`",
        "`return wave;` hands the new array back to whoever asked."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "title": "The scene asks",
      "instructions": "In `draw()`, replace `nextWave();` with `if (spawner.ready(enemies.length)) enemies = spawner.makeWave();`. In `startRound()`, replace the three wave lines with `spawner = new Spawner();`. Then delete what the spawner now does: the `let wave, wavePause, fallSpeed;` line, `spawnEnemy()`, `spawnWave()` and `nextWave()`. Last, change the HUD line in `drawTop()` from `wave` to `spawner.wave`: the old `wave` is gone, and the game would stop with `wave is not defined`.",
      "hints": [
        "A wave only arrives on a clear field, so putting the new array in `enemies` loses nothing.",
        "Search for `wavePause` and `nextWave`: when neither is found, the old wave code is gone.",
        "Search for `'wave: '` as well: that line in `drawTop()` must read `text('wave: ' + spawner.wave, 165, 26);`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "title": "The class counts",
      "instructions": "Give `Enemy` a counter that belongs to the class, as in 5.8.16 (Lab: Count your spawns): `static spawned = 0;` above the constructor and `Enemy.spawned += 1;` inside it. A static value belongs to the class, not to a round, so nothing sets it back unless the restart says so: add `Enemy.spawned = 0;` to `startRound()`. Show it on the game-over screen in `drawTop()` with `text('enemies faced: ' + Enemy.spawned, 142, 216);`, and move the R line down to 238.",
      "hints": [
        "Press R in the middle of wave 3, then lose: the count should start again from 0, not carry on from the last round."
      ]
    }
  ],
